[[:db/retract 17592186134055 :question/answer-fields 17592186130486] {:db/id 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7f21c5fc-f92d-5b81-95e0-bb89748670fa" "question-e1da803c-87de-5156-b3a7-d636001e9616" "question-0be806ce-1a6b-5ee1-964b-e362c3c8599e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133687 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d28edbbb8741260e5087e4ecc1424d03208354580d8aff6e2baeae01ba8d2c97.svg)\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c3a213f9-9ffe-56d7-a8aa-efb68c3978ef"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133686 :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\np_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad\np_2(t)=t, \\qquad\np_3(t)=t-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{ p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133688 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1, \\qquad\nf_2(t)=t, \\qquad\nf_3(t)=2t+1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t) \\: \\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f_1(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. $f_2(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{ f_1(t), f_2(t), f_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f21c5fc-f92d-5b81-95e0-bb89748670fa" :question/id #uuid "7f21c5fc-f92d-5b81-95e0-bb89748670fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-140497" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =5(1)(-1)+(1)(7)+2(1)(-1) \\\\  & =-5+7-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =5(-1)(2)+(7)(0)+2(-1)(-5) \\\\  & =-10+0+10 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =5(1)(2)+(1)(0)+2(1)(-5) \\\\  & =10+0-10 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be0c4698-714b-5480-a266-83cfdfc1738d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be0c4698-714b-5480-a266-83cfdfc1738d" :answer-field/id #uuid "be0c4698-714b-5480-a266-83cfdfc1738d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c2ae49b3-2b1a-5769-a32d-2fa88e84403a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f74c6690-ce28-580c-9560-4db51296ad3f" "answer-c2ae49b3-2b1a-5769-a32d-2fa88e84403a" "answer-db41afa9-b02c-5090-94ff-3130841b1e75" "answer-5292133c-58a4-54a4-a601-35a908997fea" "answer-821647ea-8e8f-5c03-ba00-206492b1131a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f74c6690-ce28-580c-9560-4db51296ad3f" :answer/id #uuid "f74c6690-ce28-580c-9560-4db51296ad3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2ae49b3-2b1a-5769-a32d-2fa88e84403a" :answer/id #uuid "c2ae49b3-2b1a-5769-a32d-2fa88e84403a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db41afa9-b02c-5090-94ff-3130841b1e75" :answer/id #uuid "db41afa9-b02c-5090-94ff-3130841b1e75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5292133c-58a4-54a4-a601-35a908997fea" :answer/id #uuid "5292133c-58a4-54a4-a601-35a908997fea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-821647ea-8e8f-5c03-ba00-206492b1131a" :answer/id #uuid "821647ea-8e8f-5c03-ba00-206492b1131a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134560 :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1da803c-87de-5156-b3a7-d636001e9616" :question/id #uuid "e1da803c-87de-5156-b3a7-d636001e9616" :question/math-academy-id "q-99637" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(2)(1)+3(-1)(1)+(1)(-1) \\\\  & =4-3-1 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(0)+3(1)(1)+(-1)(3) \\\\  & =0+3-3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(2)(0)+3(-1)(1)+(1)(3) \\\\  & =0-3+3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-715169d2-6248-5c13-8e14-43b51e80908b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-715169d2-6248-5c13-8e14-43b51e80908b" :answer-field/id #uuid "715169d2-6248-5c13-8e14-43b51e80908b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-518b0bf4-0290-5996-ba11-0aede8ea3071" :answer-field/choices ["answer-680ba569-6fa1-5174-a88b-6fec3325f8fd" "answer-eeedd419-cbf7-5753-9977-4da6fe0c9b81" "answer-518b0bf4-0290-5996-ba11-0aede8ea3071" "answer-c8647e45-9ab4-56a3-93bb-8a30b093366b" "answer-27c15540-3c41-5ca2-a7d9-dc8baf6f1008"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-680ba569-6fa1-5174-a88b-6fec3325f8fd" :answer/id #uuid "680ba569-6fa1-5174-a88b-6fec3325f8fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeedd419-cbf7-5753-9977-4da6fe0c9b81" :answer/id #uuid "eeedd419-cbf7-5753-9977-4da6fe0c9b81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-518b0bf4-0290-5996-ba11-0aede8ea3071" :answer/id #uuid "518b0bf4-0290-5996-ba11-0aede8ea3071" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8647e45-9ab4-56a3-93bb-8a30b093366b" :answer/id #uuid "c8647e45-9ab4-56a3-93bb-8a30b093366b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27c15540-3c41-5ca2-a7d9-dc8baf6f1008" :answer/id #uuid "27c15540-3c41-5ca2-a7d9-dc8baf6f1008" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134568 :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(4)(-8)+(-2)(4)+4(2)(5) \\\\  & =-32-8+40 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(1)+(-2)(2)+4(2)(0) \\\\  & =4-4+0 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(-8)(1)+(4)(2)+4(5)(0) \\\\  & =-8+8+0 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133446 :answer-field/correct 17592186133450 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-0be806ce-1a6b-5ee1-964b-e362c3c8599e" :question/id #uuid "0be806ce-1a6b-5ee1-964b-e362c3c8599e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140495" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(1)(3)+2(0)(1)+3(2)(-1) \\\\  & =6+0-6 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(-9)+2(0)(-2)+3(2)(3) \\\\  & =-18+0+18 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}.$ Computing the inner product, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(3)(-9)+2(1)(-2)+3(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-54-4-9 \\\\  & =-67\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nSince ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68a628e9-2427-55d0-991e-370a53971bc4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68a628e9-2427-55d0-991e-370a53971bc4" :answer-field/id #uuid "68a628e9-2427-55d0-991e-370a53971bc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2d39f349-998b-59ff-bf35-dc110bf6bf2e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d39f349-998b-59ff-bf35-dc110bf6bf2e" "answer-a6621b58-77e4-59d2-b684-96f69b011cd8" "answer-fb73d8fe-92a2-5daf-a18a-f4c0dec77755" "answer-dbb7dce8-d733-517f-ba5b-5223c54118a3" "answer-8f2d2cfb-c614-5b27-bc96-e076f598725f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d39f349-998b-59ff-bf35-dc110bf6bf2e" :answer/id #uuid "2d39f349-998b-59ff-bf35-dc110bf6bf2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6621b58-77e4-59d2-b684-96f69b011cd8" :answer/id #uuid "a6621b58-77e4-59d2-b684-96f69b011cd8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb73d8fe-92a2-5daf-a18a-f4c0dec77755" :answer/id #uuid "fb73d8fe-92a2-5daf-a18a-f4c0dec77755" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbb7dce8-d733-517f-ba5b-5223c54118a3" :answer/id #uuid "dbb7dce8-d733-517f-ba5b-5223c54118a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f2d2cfb-c614-5b27-bc96-e076f598725f" :answer/id #uuid "8f2d2cfb-c614-5b27-bc96-e076f598725f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134055 :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134559 :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t-2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-1⟩ \\\\  & =(t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0-1)+1\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,2t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(t){|}_{0}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{0}+(t){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2(0)-2)+1\\cdot (2(1)-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(0-1)\\cdot (2(0)-2)+(1-1)\\cdot (2(1)-2) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133392 :answer-field/correct 17592186133394 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3a213f9-9ffe-56d7-a8aa-efb68c3978ef" :question/id #uuid "c3a213f9-9ffe-56d7-a8aa-efb68c3978ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-104735" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(3(0))\\cdot (0+1)+(3(1))\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1){|}_{-1}+(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)^{2}-1)+(3(0))\\cdot ({0}^{2}-1)+(3(1))\\cdot ({1}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-468787f7-c1b4-5473-9896-1544ea0279e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-468787f7-c1b4-5473-9896-1544ea0279e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "468787f7-c1b4-5473-9896-1544ea0279e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-10969bc4-2424-5ec5-beef-b6411d54b752" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10969bc4-2424-5ec5-beef-b6411d54b752" "answer-414e8c9c-a038-5f39-a50b-325ec8694b78" "answer-a2d51e26-f297-5ec3-b8fc-38cafe658656" "answer-408f846a-8671-5e12-91e0-cae716143fe1" "answer-832aafe5-b074-5f94-991a-4348f32647a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10969bc4-2424-5ec5-beef-b6411d54b752" :answer/id #uuid "10969bc4-2424-5ec5-beef-b6411d54b752" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-414e8c9c-a038-5f39-a50b-325ec8694b78" :answer/id #uuid "414e8c9c-a038-5f39-a50b-325ec8694b78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2d51e26-f297-5ec3-b8fc-38cafe658656" :answer/id #uuid "a2d51e26-f297-5ec3-b8fc-38cafe658656" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-408f846a-8671-5e12-91e0-cae716143fe1" :answer/id #uuid "408f846a-8671-5e12-91e0-cae716143fe1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-832aafe5-b074-5f94-991a-4348f32647a8" :answer/id #uuid "832aafe5-b074-5f94-991a-4348f32647a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186237778 :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186237780 :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134561 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272132 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2(t-1),\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272112 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382668 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362393 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135104 :tutorial/content "Let's consider an inner product space $V$ equipped with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$ \nA set of vectors \n\n$$\n{U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis called an **orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product** if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set\n\n![](images/69/69571997d87ed3f40ee7e9b0742075fbd5ee107892f6a9b4198f75f3f5a9fce3.svg)\n\nand the inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${V}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${V}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each inner product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(2)+3(1)(2)+2(1)(-4)=2+6-8=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(-3)+3(1)(1)+2(1)(0)=-3+3+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(-3)+3(2)(1)+2(-4)(0)=-6+6+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${V}$ is an orthogonal set." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133991 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
